# Al Pastor-Style Tacos

Based on [https://www.seriouseats.com/tacos-al-pastor-recipe](https://www.seriouseats.com/tacos-al-pastor-recipe) ([wayback](http://web.archive.org/web/20251209042324/https://www.seriouseats.com/tacos-al-pastor-recipe)) and [https://www.thekitchn.com/al-pastor-tacos-recipe-23675063](https://www.thekitchn.com/al-pastor-tacos-recipe-23675063) (wayback unavailable) and [https://rainbowplantlife.com/vegan-al-pastor-tacos/](https://rainbowplantlife.com/vegan-al-pastor-tacos) (wayback unavailable)

{ rating="0" image="None" }
:::
:::

## Ingredients

### Marinade

- [ ] 3 [dried guajillo chiles]{ ing="dried-guajillo-chiles" } (or 2 Tbsp chili powder if unavailable)
- [ ] 2 [dried ancho chiles]{ ing="dried-ancho-chiles" } (or substitute more guajillo)
- [ ] 1/2 cup [pineapple juice]{ ing="pineapple-juice" } (from canned pineapple or fresh)
- [ ] 1/4 cup [white vinegar]{ ing="white-vinegar" } or apple cider vinegar
- [ ] 4 cloves [garlic]{ ing="garlic" }
- [ ] 1 tsp [ground cumin]{ ing="ground-cumin" }
- [ ] 1 tsp [dried oregano]{ ing="dried-oregano" } (Mexican oregano preferred)
- [ ] 1/2 tsp [ground cinnamon]{ ing="ground-cinnamon" }
- [ ] 1/4 tsp [ground cloves]{ ing="ground-cloves" }
- [ ] 1 tsp [salt]{ ing="salt" }
- [ ] 1/2 tsp [black pepper]{ ing="black-pepper" }
- [ ] 2 Tbsp [achiote paste]{ ing="achiote-paste" } (or 1 tsp paprika + 1/2 tsp turmeric for color)

### Protein (choose one)

- [ ] 2 lbs [pork shoulder]{ ing="pork-shoulder" }, thinly sliced (traditional)
- [ ] 2 lbs [boneless chicken thighs]{ ing="boneless-chicken-thighs" }, thinly sliced
- [ ] 2 blocks (14-16 oz each) [extra-firm tofu]{ ing="extra-firm-tofu" }, pressed and cubed (vegan)
- [ ] 2 cans (15 oz each) [pinto or black beans]{ ing="pinto-or-black-beans" }, drained (bean-based option)

### For Cooking and Serving

- [ ] 2 Tbsp [vegetable oil]{ ing="vegetable-oil" }
- [ ] 1 cup fresh [pineapple]{ ing="pineapple" }, diced (or canned, drained)
- [ ] 1/2 medium [white onion]{ ing="white-onion" }, finely diced
- [ ] 1/4 cup fresh [cilantro]{ ing="cilantro" }, chopped
- [ ] 2 [limes]{ ing="limes" }, cut into wedges
- [ ] [Corn tortillas]{ ing="corn-tortillas" } (small, street taco size)
- [ ] Optional: [salsa]{ ing="salsa" }, hot sauce, radishes, avocado

## Recipe

### Prepare the Marinade

1. If using dried [chiles]{ ing="dried-ancho-chiles" }: Remove stems and seeds, tear into pieces, and toast in a dry skillet over medium heat for 1-2 minutes until fragrant (do not burn). Soak in hot water for 15 minutes until softened
1. Add softened chiles (or chili powder), [pineapple juice]{ ing="pineapple-juice" }, [vinegar]{ ing="white-vinegar" }, [garlic]{ ing="garlic" }, [cumin]{ ing="ground-cumin" }, [oregano]{ ing="dried-oregano" }, [cinnamon]{ ing="ground-cinnamon" }, [cloves]{ ing="ground-cloves" }, [salt]{ ing="salt" }, [pepper]{ ing="black-pepper" }, and [achiote paste]{ ing="achiote-paste" } to a blender. Blend until smooth

### Marinate the Protein

1. For pork or chicken: Place sliced meat in a bowl or zip-top bag, pour marinade over, and toss to coat. Marinate for at least 2 hours or overnight in refrigerator
1. For tofu: Press tofu to remove excess moisture, cut into cubes, and marinate for at least 1 hour (or up to 8 hours)
1. For [beans]{ ing="pinto-or-black-beans" }: Toss beans with marinade and let sit for 30 minutes to 2 hours

### Cook

1. Heat [oil]{ ing="vegetable-oil" } in a large skillet or griddle over medium-high heat
1. For pork/chicken: Cook marinated meat in batches until edges are charred and meat is cooked through (6-8 minutes per batch). Remove and let rest, then chop into smaller pieces
1. For tofu: Cook cubes until edges are crispy and caramelized (8-10 minutes), turning occasionally
1. For beans: Cook beans with marinade over medium heat until liquid reduces and beans are slightly crispy on edges (8-10 minutes)
1. In the same pan, add diced [pineapple]{ ing="pineapple" } and cook until caramelized and slightly charred (3-4 minutes)

### Assemble

1. Warm [tortillas]{ ing="corn-tortillas" } on a griddle or directly over a gas flame
1. Fill tortillas with cooked protein and caramelized pineapple
1. Top with diced [onion]{ ing="white-onion" }, fresh [cilantro]{ ing="cilantro" }, and a squeeze of [lime]{ ing="limes" }
1. Serve with additional lime wedges and optional toppings

## Notes

- Traditional al pastor uses pork shoulder slow-cooked on a vertical spit (trompo) with pineapple on top, but this home version achieves similar flavors
- Achiote paste adds authentic red color and earthy flavor; find it in Mexican grocery stores or online
- For spicier tacos, add 1-2 chipotle peppers in adobo to the marinade
- Dried guajillo and ancho chiles provide the best flavor, but chili powder works as a substitute
- For extra char flavor, finish protein under the broiler for 2-3 minutes
- Leftover protein stores well in refrigerator for 3-4 days and reheats nicely
